## 2. Chalk Grid Cannot Hold Seven

**Story & Context:** At 164 BPM, Seo-jin plays a seven-attack figure using alternating open, muted, and bent geomungo notes. Malik deliberately simplifies the drum machine to kick, snare, and one rapid three-hit tom burst every second bar. The extra rhythmic space makes the seven-count rotation unusually exposed.

Min-chae plays no continuous chord part. Instead, he places one prepared-clavinet octave every four beats, acting as a fixed reference point. Hae-rin uses palm-muted janggu low-head strokes in an irregular 3+2 pattern that is intentionally shorter than both the geomungo and electronic cycles. Andre contributes tongue-slap attacks without stable pitch during the first half, then introduces one low Dorian pedal.

The central break removes the drum machine completely for eight bars while Seo-jin and Hae-rin continue their independent cycles. When the footwork beat returns on its original grid, the listener hears the same pattern as newly displaced because the acoustic accents have continued uninterrupted through the silence.

The track ends immediately after a rare moment when geomungo attack one, clavinet octave, janggu stroke, and snare coincide. Nobody repeats the coincidence.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Slate-grey chalkboard with a neat sixteen-step grid being crossed diagonally by seven repeated white strike marks that refuse to fit the boxes, small janggu stick and prepared-clavinet key nearby. Spoken fragments limited to “seven won't fit,” “keep counting,” “machine comes back,” and “once is enough.”

## 3. Suldae Lands After the Floor Has Moved

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 156 BPM and begins with almost no obvious downbeat. Malik uses isolated kicks, one clipped snare every two bars, and short sub notes separated by long gaps. Seo-jin plays a 3+3+2 geomungo grouping whose internal accents suggest an eight-attack cycle but whose phrase is deliberately carried across alternating two- and four-bar drum sections.

Min-chae uses muted clavinet ghost notes instead of chords. Hae-rin plays only tension-raised janggu strokes, avoiding the lower head entirely. Andre sustains low bass-clarinet air tones with occasional tongue attacks, creating a dark floor beneath the high-speed transients.

During the final section, Malik keeps the exact same MIDI drum pattern but changes only which kicks receive the 808 sub layer. Perceived weight shifts to new locations while the acoustic patterns remain untouched. Seo-jin responds not by changing notes but by shortening the physical damping after every third suldae strike, letting selected strings ring longer across the newly weighted kicks.

The last sound is a hard geomungo attack arriving immediately after the final electronic kick rather than with it. The string rings alone for less than two seconds before a dry cut.

